From: Marcello Barnaba <vjt@openssl.it>
Subject: modemmanager: tty hotplug honours /etc/modemmanager/ignore-tty

OpenWrt builds ModemManager without udev (-Dudev=false in the
modemmanager Makefile), so port discovery is driven entirely by these
hotplug.d shell scripts. There is no equivalent of udev's
ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE blacklist, which means MM grabs every tty it sees
on bring-up.

On the Quectel RM520N-GL that includes /dev/ttyUSB0..3 (DIAG, NMEA, AT,
AT2). The quectel-5g-tools userspace (5g-info, 5g-monitor, 5g-lock,
5g-led-bars) needs the AT port for its own queries — so we short
circuit the hotplug script when DEVNAME matches an entry in
/etc/modemmanager/ignore-tty. MM keeps managing the MHI control
surface (/dev/wwan0at0, /dev/wwan0mbim0), which is plenty for the
data plane.

The patch is applied by x3000/prepare.sh after `./scripts/feeds
install -a`. Stored as a unified diff (rather than as a files/ overlay)
so that an upstream change to 25-modemmanager-tty makes prepare.sh
fail loudly instead of silently overlaying stale logic.

--- a/feeds/packages/net/modemmanager/files/etc/hotplug.d/tty/25-modemmanager-tty
+++ b/feeds/packages/net/modemmanager/files/etc/hotplug.d/tty/25-modemmanager-tty
@@ -8,6 +8,15 @@
 # We require a device name
 [ -n "$DEVNAME" ] || exit

+# Skip ports listed in /etc/modemmanager/ignore-tty (shipped by
+# quectel-5g-tools — see openwrt-glinet-x3000 fork). They are owned by
+# other userspace and MM must never open them. OpenWrt builds MM
+# without udev so ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE is unavailable; this short
+# circuit is the equivalent.
+[ -f /etc/modemmanager/ignore-tty ] \
+    && grep -qFx "$DEVNAME" /etc/modemmanager/ignore-tty 2>/dev/null \
+    && exit 0
+
 # Always make sure the rundir exists
 mkdir -m 0755 -p "${MODEMMANAGER_RUNDIR}"

